# The same In-band RESET command, decoded in Quad I/O.
#
# Section 8.3.2, p.122, and Figure 65, p.123. The command is SIXTEEN CLOCKS,
# not two bytes, and that is the whole reason the specification states it in
# clocks: the opcode occupies eight clocks in Single I/O, four in Dual and two
# in Quad, so sixteen clocks of all ones puts FFh first whichever mode the
# target believes it is in. Section 8.3.2 says exactly that -- "the target is
# able to detect the In-band RESET command opcode regardless of the I/O mode,
# i.e. either in Single, Dual or Quad I/O configuration."
#
# So the same command is two bytes in Single, four in Dual and EIGHT IN QUAD.
#
# This file is decoded at Quad. A decoder that had hardcoded eight clocks per
# byte would read these eight bytes as sixty-four clocks and report a frame four
# times longer than Figure 65 draws, which is what makes this fixture worth
# having: it is the only place the clocks-to-bytes conversion is observable.
#
# Hand built. The capture is Single I/O throughout and never switches.
CMD FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF
